WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.910
Here is a small task.

2
00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:06.070
Given a list

3
00:00:06.070 --> 00:00:08.740
decide whether any item appears twice.

4
00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:10.460
Two programs solve it

5
00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:12.300
and both give the right answer.

6
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.560
On a list of a thousand items

7
00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:16.440
you cannot tell them apart.

8
00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.010
Both finish instantly.

9
00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:21.250
Now try a million items.

10
00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:24.330
Program A is done in a blink.

11
00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:28.630
Program B can take a minute, or even hours

12
00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:30.960
depending on the machine and the language.

13
00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.110
Same task, same correct answer.

14
00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:35.730
So what is different

15
00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:38.440
and how could we have known in advance?

16
00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:40.917
This chapter is about answering that

17
00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:43.300
question before you run anything.

18
00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:45.795
In this first video we'll see why

19
00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.730
seconds are the wrong unit

20
00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:49.507
how to count steps as a function

21
00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.962
of the input size, and how Big-O keeps only

22
00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:54.330
the part that matters.

23
00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:56.441
The obvious way to compare

24
00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:58.140
programs is to time them.

25
00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:00.910
But seconds depend on many things.

26
00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:03.130
On which machine you run

27
00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:04.835
which language you wrote it in

28
00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:06.257
and what else the computer

29
00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:07.660
is doing at the time.

30
00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:10.061
The number of steps a program takes

31
00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:12.140
does not depend on any of that.

32
00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:14.090
So we count steps.

33
00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:18.240
And since a bigger input takes more steps

34
00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:20.754
we write the count as a function

35
00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:23.170
of the input size, n.

36
00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:25.840
We call it T of n.

37
00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:28.310
Let's count for Program A.

38
00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:30.670
It compares every pair of items

39
00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:32.820
looking for two that are equal.

40
00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:35.780
With four items there are six pairs.

41
00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:37.300
With five, ten.

42
00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:39.530
With six, fifteen.

43
00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:40.710
Why?

44
00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:43.530
The first item meets all the others

45
00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:45.930
that's n minus one comparisons.

46
00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:48.810
The second meets the ones after it

47
00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:50.170
n minus two.

48
00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:52.630
And so on, down to the last item

49
00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:54.600
which has nobody left to meet.

50
00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:59.350
Adding those up gives n times n minus one

51
00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:00.400
over two.

52
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:02.441
For a thousand items

53
00:02:02.510 --> 00:02:03.839
that's almost five hundred

54
00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:05.250
thousand comparisons.

55
00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:08.200
For a million, about five hundred billion.

56
00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:10.950
Program B works differently.

57
00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:12.674
It goes through the list once

58
00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:14.639
and keeps a record of every

59
00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:16.780
item it has already seen.

60
00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:19.800
For each new item it asks one question

61
00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:21.440
have I seen this before?

62
00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.500
If yes, we have a duplicate.

63
00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:26.570
If not, it adds the item to the record.

64
00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:29.470
That's about one step per item

65
00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:30.882
so T of n is n.

66
00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:32.613
We're treating each lookup in

67
00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:34.022
the record as a single step.

68
00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:35.907
That is a fair assumption for

69
00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:37.090
the right kind of record

70
00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:40.040
and in Chapter seven we'll see exactly why.

71
00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:42.524
The exact numbers matter less

72
00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:43.550
than how they grow.

73
00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:45.860
Ask what happens when the input doubles.

74
00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:48.900
Program A: n squared over two.

75
00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:52.920
Double n and the count goes up four times.

76
00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:55.920
Program B: n steps.

77
00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:59.090
Double n and the count only doubles.

78
00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:01.730
That's the whole story behind

79
00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:02.980
the blink and the hours.

80
00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:05.860
It is the shape of the growth that decides

81
00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:07.330
not the exact count.

82
00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:09.606
Big-O notation is a way

83
00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:11.660
to say that precisely.

84
00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:14.910
Start from Program A's count and expand it.

85
00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:18.990
One half n squared, minus one half n.

86
00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:20.610
For large n

87
00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:23.470
the n squared term is far bigger

88
00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:24.820
and it dominates.

89
00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:27.660
So we drop the smaller term

90
00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:29.621
and we drop the constant factor

91
00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:30.704
because the constant depends

92
00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:32.040
on the machine anyway.

93
00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:34.370
What's left is the shape.

94
00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:38.710
We write: T of n is big-O of n squared.

95
00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:42.880
And for Program B, T of n is big-O of n.

96
00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:46.370
A few shapes come up again and again

97
00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:49.150
constant, logarithmic, linear

98
00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:50.070
and quadratic.

99
00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:52.240
You'll meet each of them in this course.

100
00:03:52.820 --> 00:03:53.793
To sum up.

101
00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:55.948
We compare algorithms by counting

102
00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:58.370
steps as a function of n

103
00:03:58.370 --> 00:03:59.860
not by timing them.

104
00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:02.660
Doubling the input is a quick test

105
00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:04.540
twice the work is linear

106
00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:06.930
four times the work is quadratic.

107
00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.522
And Big-O keeps the dominant term

108
00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:11.360
and drops the constants.

109
00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:12.928
In the next part

110
00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:15.261
we'll run both programs and check that

111
00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:17.350
the measurements match the count.

112
00:04:17.977 --> 00:04:21.032
Now let's run the two programs in Python

113
00:04:21.107 --> 00:04:22.701
and check that the count

114
00:04:22.767 --> 00:04:24.417
matches what happens.

115
00:04:24.997 --> 00:04:27.627
Here is Program A in Python.

116
00:04:27.627 --> 00:04:30.711
The outer loop picks an item, i.

117
00:04:30.807 --> 00:04:32.466
The inner loop goes through

118
00:04:32.527 --> 00:04:35.927
the items after it, starting at i plus one

119
00:04:35.927 --> 00:04:39.237
so every pair is looked at exactly once.

120
00:04:39.587 --> 00:04:42.522
If two items are equal, we return True.

121
00:04:42.597 --> 00:04:44.431
If both loops finish without

122
00:04:44.497 --> 00:04:46.827
finding a match, we return False.

123
00:04:47.407 --> 00:04:51.157
Program B keeps a set called seen.

124
00:04:51.157 --> 00:04:55.097
For each item x, if x is already in seen

125
00:04:55.097 --> 00:04:56.997
we have found a duplicate.

126
00:04:56.997 --> 00:05:00.327
Otherwise we add x to the set and move on.

127
00:05:00.677 --> 00:05:03.837
Asking whether x is in a set is the

128
00:05:03.927 --> 00:05:05.729
single step we assumed earlier.

129
00:05:05.787 --> 00:05:07.652
Chapter seven will show why Python

130
00:05:07.707 --> 00:05:09.357
can do that so quickly.

131
00:05:09.937 --> 00:05:12.412
Now let's check our count.

132
00:05:12.507 --> 00:05:14.088
This function runs the same

133
00:05:14.147 --> 00:05:15.508
two loops as Program A

134
00:05:15.567 --> 00:05:18.252
but it does nothing except count how

135
00:05:18.327 --> 00:05:20.217
often the inner body would run.

136
00:05:20.567 --> 00:05:24.377
For four items it gives six, then ten

137
00:05:24.377 --> 00:05:25.637
then fifteen.

138
00:05:25.637 --> 00:05:27.477
For a thousand items

139
00:05:27.477 --> 00:05:29.997
four hundred ninety-nine thousand

140
00:05:29.997 --> 00:05:30.818
five hundred.

141
00:05:30.877 --> 00:05:33.233
That matches n times n minus

142
00:05:33.317 --> 00:05:35.097
one over two exactly.

143
00:05:35.097 --> 00:05:37.517
For a million items we use the formula

144
00:05:37.517 --> 00:05:40.247
about five hundred billion.

145
00:05:40.827 --> 00:05:42.856
Next, the stopwatch.

146
00:05:42.957 --> 00:05:45.086
We time both programs on lists

147
00:05:45.157 --> 00:05:47.897
with no duplicate, which is the worst case

148
00:05:47.897 --> 00:05:49.587
because they have to look at everything.

149
00:05:49.937 --> 00:05:51.387
Look at the slow column.

150
00:05:51.447 --> 00:05:53.150
At one thousand items it takes

151
00:05:53.207 --> 00:05:54.767
about eight milliseconds.

152
00:05:54.767 --> 00:05:56.707
Each time we double n

153
00:05:56.707 --> 00:05:58.768
the time goes up about four times.

154
00:05:58.827 --> 00:06:00.951
At sixteen thousand items it

155
00:06:01.027 --> 00:06:02.897
is close to two seconds.

156
00:06:03.247 --> 00:06:05.617
The fast column is tiny

157
00:06:05.617 --> 00:06:08.517
and it only roughly doubles each time.

158
00:06:08.517 --> 00:06:10.741
Exactly what the step counts predicted

159
00:06:10.797 --> 00:06:12.442
even though seconds were never

160
00:06:12.497 --> 00:06:13.847
part of the argument.

161
00:06:14.427 --> 00:06:15.753
Now we can answer the question

162
00:06:15.797 --> 00:06:16.697
from the start.

163
00:06:16.757 --> 00:06:20.002
A million items is sixty-two times

164
00:06:20.097 --> 00:06:21.677
more than sixteen thousand

165
00:06:21.677 --> 00:06:25.177
so Program A does about sixty-two squared

166
00:06:25.177 --> 00:06:28.067
nearly four thousand times more work.

167
00:06:28.417 --> 00:06:32.093
Two seconds times four thousand is more

168
00:06:32.187 --> 00:06:34.747
than two hours on this machine.

169
00:06:34.747 --> 00:06:37.427
Program B does it in a blink.

170
00:06:37.427 --> 00:06:39.787
We knew that before running anything

171
00:06:39.787 --> 00:06:42.137
just from the shape of the count.

172
00:06:42.717 --> 00:06:45.242
So in Python, the counting argument and

173
00:06:45.307 --> 00:06:46.964
the stopwatch agree.

174
00:06:47.047 --> 00:06:48.802
Quadratic growth quadruples

175
00:06:48.867 --> 00:06:50.627
the time when n doubles.

176
00:06:50.627 --> 00:06:52.837
Linear growth only doubles it.

177
00:06:53.187 --> 00:06:55.880
Next time we'll make Big-O precise

178
00:06:55.957 --> 00:06:57.886
and learn how to prove a bound instead

179
00:06:57.937 --> 00:06:59.307
of just measuring one.

